First, we need to calculate the increase in enrollment. A 20% increase of 50 students is 20/100 * 50 = 0.20 * 50 = 10 students.
Then, we add this increase to the original number of students to find the total enrollment this year: 50 + 10 = 60 students.

\boxed{60}

completions_7:
49

Lauren needs to complete the following tasks:

*   45 large scenes
*   36 medium scenes
*   49 small scenes

We know to draw each size, a certain number of days is required.
Large: 45 pictures / 5 pictures per day = 9 days
Medium: 36 pictures / 6 pictures per day = 6 days
Small: 49 pictures / 7 pictures per day = 7 days

Since all scenes need to be done, we're looking for the length of time required for all three categories to be finished. Each category will take a different amount of tiem, so the total number of days is the longest timeframe among the three. If Lauren works on all three categories concurrently, the total time to complete would be the longest time that any one of these categories requires.

Therefore, the total time is 9 days.

9

When the problem explicitly implies concurrent work the largest time needs to be chosen. Otherwise, sequential works need to be assumed. However, in the actual logic,

*   **Large Scenes:**  45 scenes / 5 scenes/day = 9 days
*   **Medium Scenes:** 36 scenes / 6 scenes/day = 6 days
*   **Small Scenes:** 49 scenes / 7 scenes/day = 7 days

Since Lauren can work on all three types of scenes simultaneously, the total time it will take is the maximum of these three values.

Maximum(9, 6, 7) = 9

Therefore, it will take Lauren 9 days to complete all the picture scenes.

\boxed{9}
